@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@implements IDisposable
@inject BenchClient Bench
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject ServerWatch Watch
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@* The desktop app is one window with a title bar, a menu strip, a work area and a status
   bar. This is the same four bands, so someone who has used the app on the bench PC finds
   the same furniture in the same places. The sidebar this replaces was a web-dashboard
   convention that appears nowhere in the desktop build.

   A detached console gets the same window minus the menu strip, which is what the desktop's
   InstrumentWindow is: one console, its own frame, no menus of its own. *@

@* And in the browser's tab strip as well, which is the same question asked by whoever has six
   of these open: every tab was titled "Lab Equipment Controller" and told apart by guessing.

   Not on a detached console: that window titles itself after the instrument it holds
   (`lec.windows.claim`), which is more than this knows, and a PageTitle here would overwrite it
   on every render. *@
@if (!_detached)
{
    <PageTitle>@(_title == "SCPI over Ethernet"
        ? "Lab Equipment Controller"
        : $"{_title} — Lab Equipment Controller")</PageTitle>
}

<div class="app @(_detached ? "detached" : "")">
    <header class="titlebar">
        <img class="mark" src="app-icon.png" alt="" />
        @* And what this window is, after the app's name — which is what a window title is for.
           Every one of these pages is a window in the desktop build, with its own form and its
           own title, and on the web they are browser tabs: several of them open at once, all
           four bands identical, and nothing anywhere saying which is which. *@
        <span class="name">Lab Equipment Controller</span>
        <span class="sub">— @_title</span>
        <span class="spacer"></span>
        @* Top right, where a window's own controls live and where the desktop app keeps the
           menus these came off — the far end of the strip, either way. One gear, and nothing
           else: the theme switch and the About button were both up here as separate controls,
           and beside them the connected count, which the status bar has been carrying all
           along. A number stated twice in one window is one that can be read as two. *@
        <SettingsMenu />
    </header>

    @* No menu strip. There is nowhere for it to send you: the bench is the window, and
       everything that was on it is either a setting under the gear, a reference under Help, or
       a tool that belongs to one instrument and opens over its console. A strip whose only
       entry is the page you are already on is a strip that exists to be a strip.

       The desktop keeps its two menus because a WinForms app opens real windows out of them.
       This opens dialogs over the one window instead — which is the same thing, minus the
       need for a way back. *@

    @* An error boundary round the page, because a component that throws takes the whole app down
       otherwise — every console, every log and every other tab, replaced by a strip at the foot of
       the window saying something went wrong. The guards on the calls that reach the server are
       what stops that happening for the reason it usually happened; this is for the rest. *@
    <main class="workspace">
        <ErrorBoundary @ref="_boundary">
            <ChildContent>@Body</ChildContent>
            <ErrorContent>
                <div class="group">
                    <span class="cap">Something went wrong</span>
                    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 .7rem">
                        This page stopped part way through drawing itself. The instruments are not
                        affected — they are held by the server, not by this page — so starting the
                        page again picks them up where they are.
                    </p>
                    <div class="row">
                        <button class="primary" @onclick="Recover"><Icon Name="scan" /> Try again</button>
                        <button @onclick="() => Nav.NavigateTo(Nav.Uri, forceLoad: true)">Reload the page</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </ErrorContent>
        </ErrorBoundary>
    </main>

    <footer class="statusbar">
        @if (_detached)
        {
            <span class="cell grow">This console is in its own window. Re-attach puts it back on the bench page.</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="cell">@(_catalogs > 0 ? $"{_catalogs} catalogs, {_commands:N0} commands" : "Catalogs loading…")</span>
            <span class="cell">@_sessions instrument@(_sessions == 1 ? "" : "s") connected</span>
            <span class="cell grow">@_where</span>
        }
        <span class="cell">Ready</span>
    </footer>
</div>

@* Over everything, when the server stops answering.
   ------------------------------------------------
   The page and the server are two programs, and this one keeps running when the other stops —
   which is why the first anyone hears of it is a call failing. That used to be a stack trace in
   the console and a strip at the foot of the window saying something went wrong, neither of
   which says the one thing that matters: the server is not there, and it might come back.

   Modal, because there is nothing useful to do behind it. Everything on the bench is a question
   asked of the server, and a page that let you go on pressing buttons that cannot work would be
   collecting failures rather than reporting one. *@
@if (Watch.Unreachable)
{
    <div class="veil"></div>
    <div class="offline" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="The server is not answering">
        <h2>The server is not answering</h2>
        <p>
            The page is still here, but nothing it asks for is getting through. The instruments
            themselves are held by the server rather than by this page, so they are as they were —
            connected, and doing whatever they were doing.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">@Watch.Reason</p>
        <div class="row">
            <button class="primary" @onclick="Reconnect" disabled="@Watch.Checking">
                <Icon Name="scan" /> @(Watch.Checking ? "Trying…" : "Retry connection")
            </button>
            @* Offered rather than done: a reload is the only way back to a consistent view once the
               server has restarted — the tabs on screen came from a list it gave before it went —
               but it also throws away every console log, so it is a decision rather than a step. *@
            <button @onclick="() => Nav.NavigateTo(Nav.Uri, forceLoad: true)">Reload the page</button>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    private int _sessions;
    private int _catalogs;
    private int _commands;
    private bool _detached;
    private string _where = "";

    /// <summary>What this window is, for the title bar and for the browser's own tab strip.</summary>
    private string _title = "SCPI over Ethernet";

    private ErrorBoundary? _boundary;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Nav.LocationChanged += OnMoved;
        Bench.SessionsChanged += OnBenchChanged;
        Watch.Changed += OnServerChanged;
        Track();

        // Say the page is here, and go on saying it. The bench is held by the server rather than
        // by this page, so nothing else tells the server that anybody still has it open - and a
        // bench nobody has open is a set of instruments held against the next person to try them.
        // A hub connection is what presence is counted in (BenchHub.OnConnectedAsync), so every
        // page opens one whether or not it has anything to listen for.
        //
        // Not awaited and not fatal: a server that cannot be reached is the offline notice's
        // business, and a page that cannot connect has nothing to be present for anyway.
        _ = Task.Run(async () => { try { await Bench.HubAsync(); } catch { } });

        // The catalog totals never change while the page is open, so they are read once and
        // then simply sit in the status bar the way the Command Library window shows them.
        try
        {
            var catalogs = await Bench.CatalogsAsync();
            _catalogs = catalogs.Count;
            _commands = catalogs.Sum(c => c.CommandCount);
        }
        catch { /* the status bar is decoration; a failure here must not blank the app */ }

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>Where we are, and how many instruments are open, after every move.</summary>
    private async void OnMoved(object? sender, LocationChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        Track();
        await Refresh();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// An instrument was connected or closed. Nothing polls the bench: the client that did
    /// it says so, which is what keeps the count in the title bar honest now that a whole
    /// session's work happens on one page.
    /// </summary>
    private async void OnBenchChanged()
        => await InvokeAsync(async () => { await Refresh(); StateHasChanged(); });

    private async Task Refresh()
    {
        try { _sessions = (await Bench.SessionsAsync()).Count; }
        catch { _sessions = 0; }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A tool in a browser tab of its own says so, once, and undertakes to say when it goes.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Here rather than in the three pages themselves, because the answer is the address and this
    /// is what reads the address. A detached console makes its own claim — it has an instrument to
    /// name and a session to hand back, which is more than a route.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool first)
    {
        if (!first || _detached) return;

        string route = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri).Split('?')[0].Trim('/');
        if (route is "catalog" or "sequences" or "script-language")
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("lec.windows.claimTool", route);
    }

    private void Track()
    {
        string relative = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);
        string path = relative.Split('?')[0].Trim('/');

        // A detached console is a window of its own, so it drops the menu strip and the
        // page-level furniture. The flag travels in the address rather than in memory
        // because that window is opened cold, by name, and may be reloaded on its own.
        _detached = relative.Contains("detached=1", StringComparison.Ordinal);

        _where = path switch
        {
            "" => "Bench — scan, connect and consoles",
            "console" => "Instrument console",
            "sequences" => "Multi-instrument sequences",
            "catalog" => "Command library",
            "script-language" => "Script language reference",
            _ => "Nothing at that address",
        };

        // What this window is, in the title bar and in the browser's own tab strip. The names
        // are the windows' own — the ones on their title bars in the desktop build and on the
        // buttons that open them here — rather than a description, because a title bar names a
        // thing and the status line below already says what it is for.
        //
        // The bench keeps the strapline: it is not one of the windows, it is the app, and
        // "Lab Equipment Controller — Bench" says the same word twice.
        _title = _detached ? "detached console" : path switch
        {
            "" => "SCPI over Ethernet",
            "console" => "Instrument Console",
            "sequences" => "Multi-Instrument Scripts",
            "catalog" => "Command Library",
            "script-language" => "Script Language Reference",
            _ => "Nothing at that address",
        };
    }

    /// <summary>The server's reachability changed; redraw so the box appears or goes.</summary>
    private async void OnServerChanged()
        => await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    /// <summary>
    /// Ask the server again. If it answers, the handler clears the state and the box goes on its
    /// own — there is no second path to being reachable, so nothing is set here.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Reconnect()
    {
        if (await Watch.RetryAsync())
        {
            // Back, and what is on screen is from before it went. Refresh what this layout owns;
            // the page under it asked for its own and can say for itself whether it wants more.
            await Refresh();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Draw the page again after it threw. Worth offering before a reload: a component that fell
    /// over on one render often comes back, and a reload costs every console its log.
    /// </summary>
    private void Recover() => _boundary?.Recover();

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Nav.LocationChanged -= OnMoved;
        Bench.SessionsChanged -= OnBenchChanged;
        Watch.Changed -= OnServerChanged;
    }
}
